**user**:
> I am producing a bunch of figures in ggplot, but the editor wants to have the raw data for each plot as well (so they can presumably remake all the figures). Is there a way to extract this from the ggplot object?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can extract the raw data from a `ggplot` object. A `ggplot` object contains the associated data frame, and you can access it using the `ggplot_build()` function. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do it:
> 
> 1. Create your `ggplot` object as usual.
> 2. Use `ggplot_build()` to access the underlying data.
> 3. Depending on your plot, extract the data from the appropriate structure in the `ggplot` object.
> 
> Here's an example:
> 
> ```R
> library(ggplot2)
> 
> # Example ggplot
> p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) + geom_point()
> 
> # Extract the data used in the plot
> plot_data <- ggplot_build(p)$data[[1]]
> 
> # View the raw data
> print(plot_data)
> ```
> 
> In this example, `plot_data` would contain the data frame used for plotting, including any transformations applied during the ggplot processes. You can then export this data to a file format that the editor prefers, such as CSV, using `write.csv()`:
> 
> ```R
> write.csv(plot_data, "plot_raw_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
> ```
> 
> This will give your editor the underlying data needed to recreate the figures.
**user**:
> This is pretty good, but it shows everything in raw terms (like colors are reported as hex codes), while it would be much nicer if the legend/labels/catagorical variables were reported. Is that possible?
**assistant**:
> Yes, and this is an important distinction:  
> 
> - `ggplot_build(p)$data` gives you **stat/scale-transformed data** (what’s drawn on the screen: hex colors, numeric positions, etc.).  
> - The journal almost certainly wants the **original data** (with your factor labels, categorical variables, etc.).
> 
> You usually want to extract from the `data` slot of the ggplot object and/or the individual layers, *not* from `ggplot_build`.
> 
> ### 1. Get the original data used in the plot
> 
> If you passed a data frame to `ggplot(...)`, you can access it directly:
> 
> ```r
> p <- ggplot(df, aes(x = group, y = value, color = treatment)) +
>   geom_point()
> 
> raw_data <- p$data
> str(raw_data)
> ```
> 
> This `raw_data` will have your original factor levels, labels, etc. You can export it:
> 
> ```r
> write.csv(raw_data, "figureX_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
> ```
> 
> ### 2. If layers have their own data
> 
> Sometimes layers override `data =`:
> 
> ```r
> p <- ggplot() +
>   geom_point(data = df1, aes(x, y, color = group)) +
>   geom_smooth(data = df2, aes(x, y), method = "lm")
> ```
> 
> Then you have separate data per layer:
> 
> ```r
> layer1_data <- p$layers[[1]]$data
> layer2_data <- p$layers[[2]]$data
> ```
> 
> You can save each, or merge them with identifiers:
> 
> ```r
> layer1_data$._layer <- "points"
> layer2_data$._layer <- "smooth"
> plot_raw_data <- rbind(layer1_data, layer2_data)
> ```
> 
> ### 3. If aesthetics are mapped via `aes()`
> 
> If you used:
> 
> ```r
> ggplot(df, aes(color = treatment))
> ```
> 
> then the column `treatment` in `df` *is* the categorical variable you should deliver. You typically **don’t** want the scale-resolved hex colors at all; the editor can re-apply their own color scheme.
> 
> So, for most journal purposes, the best answer is:
> 
> > Give them the original data frame(s) that went into your ggplot call(s), possibly filtered/aggregated exactly as used for plotting, *not* `ggplot_build(p)$data`.
> 
> ### 4. Reconstructing “plot-ready” data if needed
> 
> If your plot uses `dplyr` pipes and on-the-fly transforms:
> 
> ```r
> p <- df %>%
>   filter(!is.na(y)) %>%
>   group_by(group) %>%
>   summarize(mean_y = mean(y)) %>%
>   ggplot(aes(group, mean_y)) +
>   geom_col()
> ```
> 
> Then the data you likely want to send is the summarized output:
> 
> ```r
> plot_df <- df %>%
>   filter(!is.na(y)) %>%
>   group_by(group) %>%
>   summarize(mean_y = mean(y))
> 
> write.csv(plot_df, "figureX_data.csv", row.names = FALSE)
> ```
> 
> That mirrors exactly what the figure shows and preserves categorical labels.
> 
> ---
> 
> If you show me one of your actual `ggplot` calls, I can give you a short snippet that extracts exactly the dataset the editor probably wants for that figure.
