**assistant**:
> Because in this plot there is literally no layer‑specific data yet; both layers are “waiving” back to the main `ggplot()` data and/or internal defaults.
> 
> Two key ideas:
> 
> 1. **`plot$layers[[i]]$data` only contains data if you *explicitly* supplied `data =` in that layer.**  
>    You didn’t, so each layer’s `$data` is just a **waiver**.
> 
> 2. **The actual data that gets drawn only exists *after* ggplot has been built**, i.e. after scales, stats, etc. That’s what `ggplot_build(plot)$data` gives you.
> 
> Let’s walk through your example.
> 
> ```r
> plot <- ggplot(df, aes(IDYEARS, frac, colour=VARIABLE)) +
>     geom_hline(aes(yintercept=0)) +
>     geom_line(linewidth=1)
> ```
> 
> ### What’s in `plot$data` and `plot$layers[[i]]$data`?
> 
> - `plot$data` is your `df`.
> - Each layer inherits that data **unless** you pass `data =` in the layer:
> 
> ```r
> geom_line(data = df_subset, ...)
> ```
> 
> Because you didn’t, both layers’ `$data` are just “use the default”:
> 
> ```r
> plot$layers[[1]]$data
> # list()
> # attr(,"class")
> # [1] "waiver"
> 
> plot$layers[[2]]$data
> # list()
> # attr(,"class")
> # [1] "waiver"
> ```
> 
> `waiver` is a special ggplot2 sentinel meaning “not specified; please inherit from the parent”.
> 
> So at this stage:
> 
> - There is **one** actual data frame (`df`) at `plot$data`.
> - The layers don’t carry their own copies of it; they just point back via the waiver.
> 
> ### Where does `ggplot_build(plot)$data` come from then?
> 
> When you do:
> 
> ```r
> build <- ggplot_build(plot)
> build$data
> ```
> 
> ggplot2 does the full pipeline:
> 
> 1. Start from `plot$data` (`df`) and inherited mappings (`aes(IDYEARS, frac, colour=VARIABLE)`).
> 2. For each layer:
>    - Resolve data inheritance (replace `waiver` with `plot$data` where appropriate).
>    - Apply stats, positions, transformations, etc.
>    - Produce the actual data frame used for drawing.
> 
> For your layers:
> 
> - `geom_hline(aes(yintercept = 0))`  
>   This doesn’t use `df` rows; it creates a one-row “dummy” data frame:
> 
>   ```r
>   build$data[[1]]
>   #   yintercept x y PANEL group colour size linetype alpha
>   # 1          0 NA NA     1    -1   ...   ...
>   ```
> 
> - `geom_line()`  
>   This uses the inherited `df`, turned into plotting coordinates:
> 
>   ```r
>   build$data[[2]]
>   # same number of rows as df, plus .panel, .group, scaled x/y, etc.
>   ```
> 
> But the important bit: **this constructed data lives only in the built object, not back in `plot$layers[[i]]$data`.**
> 
> ### Why your function struggles
> 
> Your code assumes:
> 
> - `plot$layers[[i]]$data` holds “raw” data for each layer.
> - `ggplot_build(plot)$data[[i]]` holds “plot” data, and you might `cbind` them.
> 
> For this plot:
> 
> - `plot$layers[[1]]$data` is a *waiver*, not a data.frame.
> - `plot$layers[[2]]$data` is also a *waiver*.
> - The only “true” original data is `plot$data` (your `df`).
> - The only “plot-ready” data is in `build$data[[1]]` and `build$data[[2]]`.
> 
> So it’s not that “ggplot forgot the data”, it’s that the design is:
> 
> - Global data at `plot$data`
> - Per‑layer data **only when you override**
> - Fully materialized per‑layer data only after `ggplot_build()`
> 
> ### What to do in practice
> 
> For the kind of export your editor wants, I’d recommend:
> 
> 1. **Prefer `plot$data` as the “raw” dataset** for the figure.
> 2. Only look at `plot$layers[[i]]$data` when it’s *not* a waiver (i.e., someone explicitly supplied `data =`).
> 3. Use `ggplot_build(plot)$data` **only** if you truly need the post‑stat/scale data.
> 
> A minimal, robust pattern:
> 
> ```r
> export_plot_data <- function(plot, path) {
>   build <- ggplot_build(plot)
> 
>   # base data (what user supplied to ggplot())
>   base_data <- plot$data
> 
>   # resolve per-layer "raw" data: fallback to base_data when data is a waiver
>   layer_raw <- lapply(plot$layers, function(lyr) {
>     if (inherits(lyr$data, "waiver")) base_data else lyr$data
>   })
> 
>   # built data (one per layer)
>   layer_built <- build$data
> 
>   # build sheets
>   sheets <- vector("list", length(plot$layers))
>   names(sheets) <- paste0("layer_", seq_along(plot$layers))
> 
>   for (i in seq_along(plot$layers)) {
>     raw   <- layer_raw[[i]]
>     built <- layer_built[[i]]
> 
>     # If no raw data (e.g. pure hline from mapping only), just use built
>     if (is.null(raw) || nrow(raw) == 0) {
>       sheets[[i]] <- built
>       next
>     }
> 
>     # If built data is empty or mismatched in length, just use raw
>     if (is.null(built) || nrow(built) == 0 || nrow(raw) != nrow(built)) {
>       sheets[[i]] <- raw
>       next
>     }
> 
>     # Otherwise, combine
>     sheets[[i]] <- cbind(raw, built)
>   }
> 
>   openxlsx::write.xlsx(sheets, file = path)
> }
> ```
> 
> For your specific plot, though, the cleanest “editor-facing” dataset is simply:
> 
> ```r
> plot_df <- df[, c("IDYEARS", "frac", "VARIABLE")]
> ```
> 
> The `geom_hline(yintercept = 0)` is just an annotation, not really “data.”
> 
