**user**:
> I also see that even though $data and plotdata have the same number of rows, they aren't ordered the same. Is there a way to infer the mapping between them?
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can, but you have to be explicit about *how* you match them. ggplot doesn’t store a direct “row index” linking `plot$data` to `ggplot_build(plot)$data[[i]]`, and stats/positions can reorder rows. So any mapping has to be inferred from variables that uniquely identify each row.
> 
> ### 1. When a clean mapping is possible
> 
> If the layer is essentially `stat_identity` (no aggregation) and you haven’t dropped/duplicated rows, then the built data will typically be a permutation of the original data with extra columns (like `x`, `y`, `PANEL`, `group`, etc.).
> 
> In that case you can:
> 
> 1. Define a set of *key columns* that together uniquely identify each row in the original data.
> 2. Use those keys to join the built data back to the original data.
> 
> For example, with your plot:
> 
> ```r
> p <- ggplot(df, aes(IDYEARS, frac, colour=VARIABLE)) +
>   geom_line(linewidth=1)
> ```
> 
> The natural key is `IDYEARS` + `VARIABLE` (assuming one row per combo):
> 
> ```r
> build <- ggplot_build(p)
> layer_built <- build$data[[1]]  # layer for geom_line
> 
> # Raw data (inherited from p$data)
> raw <- p$data
> 
> # Choose key columns that uniquely identify rows
> keys <- c("IDYEARS", "VARIABLE")
> 
> # Check for uniqueness as a sanity check
> stopifnot(nrow(raw) == nrow(dplyr::distinct(raw, !!!rlang::syms(keys))))
> stopifnot(nrow(layer_built) == nrow(dplyr::distinct(layer_built, !!!rlang::syms(keys))))
> 
> # Join built data back to raw data in a way that reorders built to match raw
> merged <- dplyr::left_join(
>   raw,
>   layer_built,
>   by = keys,
>   suffix = c(".raw", ".built")
> )
> ```
> 
> Now `merged` is in the same row order as `raw`, but includes all the plotting variables (scaled `x`, `y`, etc.) from `layer_built`. The key columns act as your inferred mapping.
> 
> If you’re not using dplyr:
> 
> ```r
> merged <- merge(raw, layer_built, by = c("IDYEARS", "VARIABLE"), sort = FALSE)
> # this may still reorder; you can re-sort merged to match raw if needed:
> idx <- match(
>   interaction(raw$IDYEARS, raw$VARIABLE),
>   interaction(merged$IDYEARS, merged$VARIABLE)
> )
> merged <- merged[idx, ]
> ```
> 
> ### 2. General pattern for a function
> 
> You can turn that idea into a helper that takes a ggplot object and a set of key columns:
> 
> ```r
> map_raw_to_built <- function(plot, layer = 1, keys) {
>   build <- ggplot_build(plot)
>   built <- build$data[[layer]]
> 
>   # Determine raw data for this layer: inherit from plot$data if waiver
>   lyr <- plot$layers[[layer]]
>   if (inherits(lyr$data, "waiver")) {
>     raw <- plot$data
>   } else {
>     raw <- lyr$data
>   }
> 
>   # Check that keys exist
>   if (!all(keys %in% names(raw)) || !all(keys %in% names(built))) {
>     stop("Some key columns not found in both raw and built data.")
>   }
> 
>   # Optional: sanity checks for uniqueness
>   # if (nrow(raw) != nrow(unique(raw[keys]))) stop("Keys not unique in raw.")
>   # if (nrow(built) != nrow(unique(built[keys]))) stop("Keys not unique in built.")
> 
>   # Merge and restore raw’s row order
>   merged <- merge(raw, built, by = keys, all.x = TRUE, sort = FALSE)
>   idx <- match(
>     do.call(interaction, raw[keys]),
>     do.call(interaction, merged[keys])
>   )
>   merged[idx, ]
> }
> ```
> 
> Usage for your plot:
> 
> ```r
> merged <- map_raw_to_built(plot, layer = 2, keys = c("IDYEARS", "VARIABLE"))
> ```
> 
> Layer 1 (`geom_hline`) doesn’t really map to `df` rows at all, so you’d ignore it.
> 
> ### 3. When it is *not* really possible
> 
> There are cases where you cannot sensibly define a 1:1 mapping:
> 
> - **Aggregating stats**: `stat_smooth()`, `stat_summary()`, `geom_boxplot()`, etc. combine multiple raw rows into a single built row (or a few). Then the mapping is many-to-one or many-to-few, not a permutation.
> - **Jitter/dodge with grouping**: sometimes the built data includes duplicated or expanded rows (e.g., count-based stats, binning).
> - **Facets**: still usually OK if your key columns include the facet variables, but be careful.
> 
> In those cases, you can either:
> 
> - Accept that the mapping is *conceptual* (e.g., “this smoothed line comes from all rows in group X”), or
> - Export two datasets:
>   - “Raw data for the figure” (the original df you think of as your scientific data).
>   - “Derived plotting data” (e.g., the smoothed line coordinates from `ggplot_build`).
> 
> For a journal, that’s often what they actually want.
> 
> ### 4. Recommendation for your situation
> 
> For your line plot:
> 
> - The cleanest mapping is on `IDYEARS` + `VARIABLE`.
> - Use that to merge `df` with `ggplot_build(plot)$data[[2]]` (the line layer), and ignore the hline layer entirely.
> 
> If you’d like, paste the structure of `str(df)` and `head(ggplot_build(plot)$data[[2]])`, and I can give you a very compact, concrete snippet that does the join and preserves exactly the ordering you prefer.
